  "full_response": "Git's handling of branching and merging is one of its core strengths, allowing for flexible and efficient workflows. Here's an overview of how Git manages these operations internally:\n\n### Branching\n\n- **References to Commits**: In Git, a branch is essentially a lightweight movable reference to a commit. It is stored as a pointer in the `.git/refs/heads/` directory. This makes branching very quick, almost instantaneous, as it does not involve copying any files or data.\n  \n- **HEAD**: The `HEAD` reference points to the current branch checked out. When you switch branches, Git updates `HEAD` to point to the new branch.\n\n- **Commit History**: Each branch maintains its own commit history. When you make commits on a branch, it creates new commit nodes in the commit history tree, branching off from the previous commit.\n\n### Merging\n\n- **Merge Commit**: Git often creates a new commit, called a merge commit, to combine changes from different branches. This commit has two parents: one being the last commit from the current branch and the other from the branch being merged into it.\n\n- **Fast-Forward Merge**: If the branch being merged into does not have new commits after the branch being merged from, Git can perform a fast-forward merge. In this case, `HEAD` is simply moved forward to point to the latest commit of the other branch, without creating a new merge commit.\n\n- **3-Way Merge**: For non-fast-forward merges, Git performs a 3-way merge, which involves:\n  1. **Common Ancestor**: Finding the last common ancestor of the two branches.\n  2. **Three Trees**:\n     - The content of the first branch's tip.\n     - The content of the second branch's tip.\n     - The content of the common ancestor.\n  3. **Conflict Resolution**: Comparing these three trees to automatically reconcile changes. When automatic merging cannot be done, it creates conflicts that need to be manually resolved in the workspace.\n\n- **Detached HEAD**: When merging, if Git's internal state (commit tree) results in a temporary state not associated with a branch, it might leave the repository in a \"detached HEAD\" state. This can occur if merging branches in detached HEAD mode. To resolve this, you typically need to create a new branch from the merged state.\n\n### Special Merge Strategies\n\n- **Recursive Merge**: Default strategy used by Git for merging non-fast-forward branches. It can handle complex merge scenarios by recursively analyzing three-way",
